Actually, GDP is flawed. As an expert in national accounting reconstructions, I argue that it understates the significant improvements in wellbeing humanity has achieved. While GDP captures short-term cycles, movements, and level comparisons, it fails to account for factors like the value of good quality, willingness to pay, and the overall quality of life.

The challenge with better measures is that they are difficult to develop universally and can't cover all societies. Therefore, GDP remains a reasonable indicator given its limitations.

