People sometimes ask why fine-tune when general-purpose models keep improving. Bridgewater's work shows that with the right data—specifically, expert judgments—you can significantly outperform prompting-only approaches.

@ddkang and the Bridgewater AIA Labs team are great, and it's good to see them sharing this.

Sorting which financial documents are worth an analyst's time is surprisingly difficult for frontier LLMs. By using an expert-labeled dataset and on-policy distillation, Bridgewater fine-tuned a model to do this reliably and at low cost.

Political superintelligence is emerging as Google releases systematic evidence on how citizens use AI to assist with government tasks. The most common civic-related request is paying taxes, but users also seek help understanding voting procedures, immigration rules, navigating bureaucratic services, and more.

Many users access these features after business hours, when they can't get help from the bureaucracy itself. Most activity involves chatting with AI, hinting at a future where agents carry out bureaucratic tasks and represent us in daily life. This role for AI is both exciting and underrated.

